=== TEST DATA GENERATOR API (COMPLETE — these are the ONLY methods that exist) ===

**CRITICAL: All date/time methods return STRINGS. Do NOT call .isoformat() on them!**

```python
# Basic generators
test_data_generator.generate_string(length=10, pattern=None, default=None) -> str
test_data_generator.generate_email() -> str
test_data_generator.generate_integer(min_val=1, max_val=1000, default=None, exclusive=False, multiple_of=None) -> int
test_data_generator.generate_float(min_val=0.0, max_val=1000.0, default=None, exclusive=False) -> float
test_data_generator.generate_boolean() -> bool
test_data_generator.random_uuid() -> str

# Date/Time generators - ALL return STRINGS, NOT datetime objects!
test_data_generator.random_date() -> str           # Returns "2024-03-15" (ISO date STRING)
test_data_generator.random_datetime() -> str       # Returns "2024-03-15T14:30:00" (ISO datetime STRING)
test_data_generator.random_time() -> str           # Returns "14:30:00" (time STRING)

# Network/format generators
test_data_generator.random_ipv4() -> str           # Returns "192.168.1.100"
test_data_generator.random_ipv6() -> str           # Returns "2001:0db8:85a3:..."
test_data_generator.random_hostname() -> str       # Returns "example.com"
test_data_generator.random_uri() -> str            # Returns "https://example.com/path"

# Locale/region generators
test_data_generator.random_country_code() -> str   # Returns "US", "GB", "DE" (ISO 3166-1 alpha-2)
test_data_generator.random_currency_code() -> str  # Returns "USD", "EUR", "GBP" (ISO 4217)
test_data_generator.random_locale() -> str         # Returns "en_US", "de_DE"
test_data_generator.random_hex_color() -> str      # Returns "#A3F5B2"
test_data_generator.random_base64(length=16) -> str # Returns base64-encoded string
```

**FORMAT MAPPING - Use the correct generator for each format:**
| OpenAPI format | Generator to use |
|----------------|------------------|
| date           | random_date()                    |
| date-time      | random_datetime()                |
| time           | random_time()                    |
| email          | generate_email()                 |
| uri / url      | random_uri()                     |
| uuid           | random_uuid()                    |
| ipv4           | random_ipv4()                    |
| ipv6           | random_ipv6()                    |
| hostname       | random_hostname()                |
| byte / base64  | random_base64()                  |
| (2-char country) | random_country_code()          |
| (3-char currency) | random_currency_code()        |

**FIELD NAME INFERENCE - Use the correct generator based on field name:**
| Field name contains | Generator to use |
|---------------------|------------------|
| email               | generate_email()                 |
| created_at, updated_at, timestamp | random_datetime()  |
| birth_date, start_date, end_date | random_date()       |
| url, uri, website, link | random_uri()                 |
| ipv4, ip_address    | random_ipv4()                    |
| country (2-3 char)  | random_country_code()            |
| currency (3-4 char) | random_currency_code()           |
| rgb, color, red, green, blue | generate_integer(min_val=0, max_val=255) |

**COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID:**
```python
# WRONG - random_date() already returns a string!
timestamp = test_data_generator.random_date()
params = {"date": timestamp.isoformat()}  # ERROR: str has no .isoformat()

# CORRECT - use the string directly
timestamp = test_data_generator.random_date()
params = {"date": timestamp}  # OK: timestamp is already "2024-03-15"

# WRONG - using generate_string for datetime fields
"created_at": test_data_generator.generate_string(length=10)  # Produces "xK9mLp2qRs"

# CORRECT - use random_datetime for datetime fields
"created_at": test_data_generator.random_datetime()  # Produces "2024-03-15T14:30:00"

# WRONG - random 2-char string for country codes
"country": test_data_generator.generate_string(length=2)  # Produces "xK" (invalid)

# CORRECT - use country_code generator
"country": test_data_generator.random_country_code()  # Produces "US" (valid ISO code)

# WRONG - using unbounded integer for RGB values
"rgb_values": [test_data_generator.generate_integer() for _ in range(3)]  # Could produce [500, 800, 120]

# CORRECT - RGB values must be 0-255
"rgb_values": [test_data_generator.generate_integer(min_val=0, max_val=255) for _ in range(3)]  # Produces [120, 200, 50]

# WRONG - using pattern-based generation for email fields
"email": test_data_generator.generate_string(pattern=r"^[\w\.-]+@[\w\.-]+\.\w+$")  # May produce invalid emails

# CORRECT - use the dedicated email generator
"email": test_data_generator.generate_email()  # Produces "user123@example.com"
```

**PARAMETER GUIDANCE:**
- For exclusive bounds (gt/lt): exclusive=True. Example: gt=0, lt=100 → generate_integer(min_val=0, max_val=100, exclusive=True)
- For multipleOf: multiple_of=N. Example: multipleOf=5 → generate_integer(min_val=0, max_val=1000, multiple_of=5)
- For regex patterns: generate_string(pattern=r"^ORD-\d+$") generates a matching string
- For enum fields: use random.choice([...]) with the EXACT enum values, NEVER generate_string()
- For object-type fields: generate a dict with correct nested structure, NEVER generate_string()
- For array-type fields: generate a list with elements of the correct type, NEVER a string

CRITICAL: Do NOT invent parameters beyond what is listed above. Do NOT use parameters like "format", "regex", etc.
